    "q-350586": {
      "dom_id": "step-q350586",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the sum of the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n$1+\\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{25}{81}+⋯+\\frac{{5}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}+⋯=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The first thing we notice is that we can rewrite the terms of the series as powers of $\\frac{5}{9}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{25}{81}+⋯+\\frac{{5}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}+⋯ & =1+\\frac{{5}^{1}}{{9}^{1}}+\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{9}^{2}}+⋯+\\frac{{5}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{1}+{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}+⋯+{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{5}{9})} & =1+{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{1}+{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}+{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{3}+⋯+{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{n}+⋯ \\\\ \\frac{9}{4} & =1+\\frac{5}{9}+\\frac{25}{81}+\\frac{125}{729}+⋯+\\frac{{5}^{n}}{{9}^{n}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\frac{9}{4}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
