    "q-125964": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125964",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the sum of the series $1+\\frac{e}{3}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{9}+⋯+\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯.$",
      "worked_solution": "The first thing we notice is that we can rewrite the terms of the series as powers of $\\frac{e}{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{e}{3}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{9}+⋯+\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯ & =1+\\frac{{e}^{1}}{{3}^{1}}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+⋯+\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+{(\\frac{e}{3})}^{1}+{(\\frac{e}{3})}^{2}+⋯+{(\\frac{e}{3})}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{e}{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{e}{3})} & =1+\\frac{e}{3}+{(\\frac{e}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{e}{3})}^{3}+⋯+{(\\frac{e}{3})}^{n}+⋯ \\\\ \\frac{3}{3-e} & =1+\\frac{e}{3}+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{e}^{3}}{27}+⋯+\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{3}^{n}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\frac{3}{3-e}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
