    "q-50915": {
      "dom_id": "step-q50915",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$1+\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{1}{49}+⋯+\\frac{1}{{7}^{n}}+⋯=$",
      "worked_solution": "The first thing we notice is that we can rewrite the terms of the series as powers of $\\frac{1}{7}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{1}{49}+⋯+\\frac{1}{{7}^{n}}+⋯ & =1+\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{2}}+⋯+\\frac{1}{{7}^{n}}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{7}+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}+⋯+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{7}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{7})} & =1+\\frac{1}{7}+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{3}+⋯+{(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n}+⋯ \\\\ \\frac{7}{6} & =1+\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{1}{49}+\\frac{1}{343}+⋯+\\frac{1}{{7}^{n}}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\frac{7}{6}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
